      "target": {
        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "fix(ai): avoid websocket cache clobber on stale reused release",
        "body": "Closes #105434\n\n## What Problem This Solves\nReleasing a reused ChatGPT Responses WebSocket deleted `websocketSessionCache` by session id without checking whether a newer same-session connection already owned that slot. A stale release could close/evict a valid replacement connection.\n\n## Why This Change Was Made\nThe new-connection release path already guarded deletion with an identity check (`cache.get(sessionId) === entry`). The reused-lease release path and idle-expiry callback did not, so a replacement-before-release race could clobber the newer socket. This extends the same ownership guard to those async teardown paths.\n\n## User Impact\nCached WebSocket sessions are less likely to drop a healthy replacement connection when an older reused lease later releases or expires. No config or API changes.\n\n## Evidence\n- Source: `packages/ai/src/providers/openai-chatgpt-responses.ts` reused `release` + `scheduleSessionWebSocketExpiry` now delete only when the cache still references the captured entry (mirrors the existing new-connection release guard).\n- Test: added replacement-before-release coverage in `packages/ai/src/providers/openai-chatgpt-responses.test.ts`.\n- Proof: `node scripts/run-vitest.mjs packages/ai/src/providers/openai-chatgpt-responses.test.ts` — 33 passed (Testbox warmup unavailable; local Vitest fallback).\n- Autoreview: blocked in this environment (`codex` executable not found after secret-clean bundle prep).\n\nMade with [Cursor](https://cursor.com)",
